  "final_response": "Let $G$ be a group of order $45 = 3^2 \\cdot 5$. By Sylow's theorems, the number $n_3$ of Sylow 3-subgroups satisfies $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$ and $n_3 \\mid 5$, so $n_3 = 1$. Hence the unique Sylow 3-subgroup $P$ is normal in $G$. Similarly, $n_5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$ and $n_5 \\mid 9$ gives $n_5 = 1$, so the unique Sylow 5-subgroup $Q$ is normal in $G$. Since $P \\cap Q = \\{e\\}$ (orders coprime) and $|P||Q| = 45 = |G|$, we have $G \\cong P \\times Q$. Groups of order $p^2$ are abelian, so $P \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_9$ or $\\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_3$, and $Q \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_5$. Thus $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_9 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_5 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_{45}$ or $G \\cong (\\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_3) \\times \\mathbb{Z}_5 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_5$. These are non-isomorphic: the first is cyclic, the second has exponent 15 and no element of order 45. Hence there are exactly two isomorphism classes of groups of order 45.",
